+<!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- info tips -->
+<!-- Shown by hovering the (i) icons; one floating tip, so no column clips it. -->
+<template id="tip-vae">
+  <span class="tip-kind">VAE &middot; sound decoder</span>
+  <p>The last step of every song. The model writes the song as a compact description, and the VAE
+    turns it into audio. It changes the tone and clarity, not the notes. One per song.</p>
+  <dl data-fill="vaes">
+    <dt>Standard</dt><dd><code>m-a-p/YuE2-Vae</code><br />current official, best sound</dd>
+    <dt>Legacy</dt><dd><code>m-a-p/YuE2-Vae-legacy</code><br />older official, used for the published scores</dd>
+    <dt>Blend</dt><dd><code>Mothersuperior/YuE2-Vae-merge-0.666</code><br />community mix, &#8532; Standard + &#8531; Legacy</dd>
+  </dl>
+  <p>A finished song can get another VAE version from its page, in seconds.</p>
+</template>
+<template id="tip-sliders">
+  <span class="tip-kind">LoRA plugin &middot; <code>ntc-ai/yue2-particle-sliders</code></span>
+  <p>Small add-ons that nudge the song model toward a voice or a genre while it writes the music,
+    so they change what is played, not just how it sounds.</p>
+  <p>Tap to add; 0 is off, 1 is full; stack as many as you like. Trained in Direct mode; applied only
+    while the music tokens are written, never to the score or the sound. Experimental.</p>
+</template>
+<template id="tip-precision">
+  <span class="tip-kind">Model precision</span>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>BF16</dt><dd>the weights as stored in the backbone file (the release: about 3 significant
+      digits per number); matrix products round their inputs to 16 bits; fastest</dd>
+    <dt>F32</dt><dd>the same weights, widened exactly as they load, and everything in between kept
+      at full precision (about 7 digits); about twice the GPU memory for the weights, and slower</dd>
+  </dl>
+  <p>The author of this C++ port reports clearer, less misspoken words with full-precision maths.
+    Measured here: F32 matches the PyTorch FP32 model to about 0.02%. Switching reloads the model
+    with the next song; compare a few songs each way.</p>
+</template>
+<template id="tip-model">
+  <span class="tip-kind">Backbone file</span>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>BF16</dt><dd>the release weights, as published (about 7.2 GB)</dd>
+    <dt>Q8_0 &hellip;</dt><dd>smaller quantized copies: less memory, slightly different numbers</dd>
+  </dl>
+  <p>Switching drops the old file from memory; the next song loads the new one.</p>
+</template>
+<template id="tip-context">
+  <span class="tip-kind">Context size &middot; key/value cache</span>
+  <p>0 keeps the whole 24,576 rows. Smaller saves GPU memory, but it must hold the prompt, the
+    score and the song: roughly 2,500 rows plus 25 for every second of music.</p>
+  <p>VAE tile frames: the decoder works on tiles of this size. Smaller tiles need less memory at the
+    same speed; 512 is the reference setting.</p>
+</template>
+<template id="tip-keep">
+  <span class="tip-kind">Keep models loaded</span>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Off</dt><dd>each stage loads, runs and leaves GPU memory; the card is free again after every song</dd>
+    <dt>On</dt><dd>everything stays resident, so the next song starts at once</dd>
+  </dl>
+  <p>The C++ counterpart of the other console's "offload" choice.</p>
+</template>
+<template id="tip-transcribe">
+  <span class="tip-kind">Transcription models</span>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>MERT</dt><dd><code>m-a-p/MERT-v2-FullSong</code><br />listens to the recording</dd>
+    <dt>SheetSage</dt><dd><code>m-a-p/SheetSage2</code><br />writes what it hears as a score</dd>
+  </dl>
+  <p>Both run inside the server from one converted file, in its queue like a song. The score loads
+    into "Supply your own score" and the song model follows it. WAV and MP3 go up as they are;
+    other formats are converted in the browser first.</p>
+</template>
+<template id="tip-seeds">
+  <span class="tip-kind">Two seeds</span>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Music</dt><dd>the token draw: the score, the melody and the length come from this one</dd>
+    <dt>Sound</dt><dd>the acoustic noise: the same music played through a different grain</dd>
+  </dl>
+  <p>Blank rolls a random one. Every take keeps both, so it can be replayed exactly, or given a
+    new sound in seconds without writing the music again.</p>
+</template>
+<template id="tip-output">
+  <span class="tip-kind">Output</span>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>WAV 24</dt><dd>the fair comparison with the other console's 24-bit files</dd>
+    <dt>WAV 32</dt><dd>raw float, no normalisation</dd>
+    <dt>MP3</dt><dd>small files; the server's own default</dd>
+    <dt>Peak clip</dt><dd>normalises to the peak left after ignoring this many samples per million;
+      0 is the true peak</dd>
+  </dl>
+  <p>Sound variations render the same music with seeds sound&nbsp;+&nbsp;1, +&nbsp;2&hellip; side by side.</p>
+</template>
+<template id="tip-versions">
+  <span class="tip-kind">Versions</span>
+  <p>Separate songs from the same prompt, each with its own music seed (seed, seed&nbsp;+&nbsp;1&hellip;).
+    The server writes up to <strong data-fill="max-batch">1</strong> side by side in one pass; more are
+    queued as further passes.</p>
+</template>
+<script src="examples.js"></script>
+<script src="app.js"></script>
+</body>
+</html>
